Euthyroid Hashimoto's Disease
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Palpable mild goiter, positive antithyroid antibodies, asymptomatic

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-017
Reason for Visit: Acute severe anxiety, choking sensation, and globus pharyngeus requiring exclusion of acute airway compromise.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute subjective dysphagia and a sensation of throat constriction in a patient with a known thyroid mass. Immediate clinical review was necessary to exclude acute goiter hemorrhage, tracheal deviation, or impending airway obstruction prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My throat feels incredibly tight, like someone is choking me. I can feel a lump there and I'm terrified it's suddenly growing and cutting off my air.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports normal thyroid function with autoimmune markers on recent routine testing. Tonight, the patient felt an acute sensation of a lump in the throat (globus pharyngeus), precipitating severe panic. Over the last two hours, the patient developed a subjective feeling of choking, hyperventilation, and difficulty swallowing saliva. The patient has been continuously palpating the anterior neck, exacerbating the discomfort.
Functional Status: Independent
Social/Safety Concerns: High acute anxiety.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/90 | HR: 105 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Alert, tachycardic, speaking in full uninterrupted sentences, no stridor.
Physical Exam: Neck examination reveals a palpable mild goiter, smooth and non-tender to light touch. No erythema or localized heat. Trachea is strictly central. No cervical lymphadenopathy. History confirms positive antithyroid antibodies, asymptomatic from a metabolic standpoint. Respiratory exam is completely clear. The patient can swallow water easily during the exam despite subjective difficulty with saliva.
Environmental Observations: Safe, brightly lit environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No thyroid hormone replacement therapy currently required.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Globus Pharyngeus and Acute Anxiety secondary to Euthyroid Hashimoto's Disease - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Acute Airway Obstruction / Goiter Hemorrhage - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of stridor, ability to speak in full sentences, central trachea, and normal oxygen saturation.

Thyrotoxic Crisis (Thyroid Storm) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal temperature, absence of rigorous tremors, and known euthyroid state.

Acute Anaphylaxis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of urticaria, facial angioedema, wheeze, or hemodynamic instability.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute subjective choking and dysphagia, heavily mimicking an acute mechanical airway obstruction. Triage mandated immediate physical evaluation to exclude rapid goiter expansion or structural airway compromise. Clinical examination definitively established a central, patent airway without stridor, identifying the presentation as an acute anxiety response (globus sensation) triggered by hypervigilance over a benign, chronic goiter. The rapid bedside exclusion of an airway emergency was essential to immediately de-escalate the patient's panic, avoiding an unnecessary lights-and-sirens ambulance dispatch and satisfying the criteria for urgent intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided regarding the structural safety of the airway. Demonstrated swallowing mechanics with a glass of water.
Positive Behavioral Change: Stop pressing on your neck immediately and take small, slow sips of ice water over the next 10 minutes. Removing external pressure and swallowing cold fluid will break the muscle tension in your throat, instantly proving that your airway is completely open.
Home-Home Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP appointment in 7 days for psychological support and routine ultrasound discussion.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if you develop a high-pitched whistling sound when you breathe in (stridor), or if your lips turn blue.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The mild goiter changes very slowly over years, not minutes. The tightness you feel is muscle tension from anxiety, not the gland suddenly swelling.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if your voice suddenly becomes very hoarse, or if you become completely unable to swallow liquids.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your thyroid is slightly enlarged due to antibodies, but it is working perfectly and not blocking your breathing. The choking feeling is entirely driven by stress-related muscle spasms in the neck.
Actionable Steps: Keep your hands away from your neck to prevent bruising the area. Practice deep, slow breathing until your heart rate settles down.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Anxiety-related throat tightness. Airway is completely clear and safe. Thyroid is stable. Stop touching the neck. See your GP next week.

SMS EDUCATION

Drink cold water slowly and stop pressing your neck. Go directly to ED if you develop a noisy, whistling breath or if your lips turn blue.
